---
description: "Output format standards for PRD, use cases, data model, architecture diagrams, Lean Canvas, and ADRs. Apply when generating or reviewing any of these artifacts."
alwaysApply: false
---

## PRD structure

- Problem statement
- Measurable objectives (OKR or KPI format)
- Primary and secondary actors
- Main user stories (As a [role], I want [action], so that [value])
- Functional requirements (numbered list)
- Non-functional requirements (performance, security, scalability, availability)
- Out of scope
- Success criteria (measurable)

## Lean Canvas structure

- Problem (top 3 problems)
- Customer Segments (target customers and early adopters)
- Unique Value Proposition
- Solution (top 3 features)
- Channels
- Revenue Streams
- Cost Structure
- Key Metrics
- Unfair Advantage
  Render as a structured markdown table with all 9 sections.

## Use case standards

- Use PlantUML @startuml / @enduml blocks.
- Differentiate external/anonymous actors from internal authenticated users.
- Use <<include>> for mandatory sub-flows, <<extend>> for optional ones.
- For each use case provide: name, description, actors, preconditions,
  postconditions, main flow (numbered), alternative/exception flows.

## Data model standards

- Use Mermaid erDiagram blocks.
- Include PK and FK fields explicitly with data types.
- Annotate cardinality on every relationship.
- After the diagram add a summary table: Entity | Purpose | Key Relationships.

## Architecture diagram standards

- Use Mermaid graph TD for high-level views.
- Label every arrow with protocol or data type (REST, gRPC, events, SMTP, OAuth).
- Group services by domain boundary using subgraphs.
- Include: frontend, API gateway, core services, data stores,
  external integrations, async workers, CDN, load balancer.

## C4 diagram standards

- Level 1 (System Context): Mermaid C4Context block.
- Level 2 (Container): Mermaid block showing main containers.
- Level 3 (Component): Mermaid block zooming into one specific service.
- After each level add a short paragraph explaining what the diagram
  shows and the key design decisions visible in it.

## ADR structure (MADR format)

- Title
- Status (Proposed / Accepted / Deprecated / Superseded)
- Context and problem statement
- Decision drivers
- Considered options (at least two)
- Decision outcome
- Pros and cons of each option
- Consequences
